  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on skewness, specifically preferring the lottery with more probability on unfavorable outcomes (more right-skewed). In this case, that would be Lottery B (80% on -4000). However, the individual’s reasoning does not reflect an analysis of distributional asymmetry or a deliberate preference for more unfavorable outcomes. Instead, the individual is attracted to the small chance of a better outcome (0 loss), which is the opposite of what a 'right-skewness' reason would emphasize. The reason would prefer B not because of the 20% chance of 0, but because 80% is on the worst outcome—yet the individual is focusing on the small chance of a good result, not the high chance of a bad one. There is no mention of or apparent attention to the concept of skewness or the concentration of probability on bad outcomes. The justification is more consistent with a 'hope for the best' or 'chance to avoid loss' heuristic, not a preference for right-skewed distributions. Therefore, the decision reason was not used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 10
